Stack combinators on the infinite product: fold, unfold, push, head, and tail, with the usual head/tail/push laws.
<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`fold`).** Folding a pair consisting of an element and a sequence into a single sequence is the inverse of the product–sequence isomorphism.

*Proof.* By definition as the inverse isomorphism. ∎

**Theorem (`iterProdIso_apply`).** The product–sequence isomorphism sends a sequence to the pair of its zeroth component with the sequence of remaining components.

*Proof.* By the definition of the isomorphism. ∎

**Definition (`push`).** The push combinator places an element at the head of a sequence; it is the same map as fold.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Definition (`unfold`).** Unfolding a sequence recovers the pair of its head with its tail sequence, via the product–sequence isomorphism.

*Proof.* By definition as the forward isomorphism. ∎

**Definition (`head`).** The head of a sequence is the first factor of its unfolding.

*Proof.* Compose the first projection with unfold. ∎

**Theorem (`head_apply`).** Evaluating head at a sequence yields the zeroth component.

*Proof.* Unpack the composition and the isomorphism, then take the first factor of the resulting pair. ∎

**Definition (`tail`).** The tail of a sequence is the second factor of its unfolding.

*Proof.* Compose the second projection with unfold. ∎

**Theorem (`tail_apply`).** Evaluating tail at a sequence yields the sequence of components shifted by one.

*Proof.* Unpack the composition and the isomorphism, then take the second factor of the resulting pair. ∎

**Theorem (`component_tail`).** The $n$-th component of a tail is the $(n{+}1)$-st component of the original sequence.

*Proof.* Apply the component formula for sequences built from a function of indices. ∎

**Theorem (`fold_unfold`).** Folding the unfolding of a sequence recovers the sequence.

*Proof.* The two directions of the product–sequence isomorphism cancel. ∎

**Theorem (`unfold_fold`).** Unfolding the folding of a pair recovers the pair.

*Proof.* The two directions of the product–sequence isomorphism cancel. ∎

**Theorem (`push_head_tail`).** Pushing the head and tail of a sequence recovers the sequence.

*Proof.* Reduce push to fold, reassemble the pair of projections from the unfolding, and apply fold–unfold. ∎

**Theorem (`head_push`).** The head of a pushed pair is the pushed element.

*Proof.* Reduce to unfold–fold and the first projection of a pair. ∎

**Theorem (`tail_push`).** The tail of a pushed pair is the pushed sequence.

*Proof.* Reduce to unfold–fold and the second projection of a pair. ∎
